NAME
    screen - the on-screen text console (glass TTY): off, on, or report

SYNOPSIS
    screen off      stop mirroring the console onto the GL screen
    screen on       mirror again (clears the screen and homes the cursor first)
    screen          report the current state

DESCRIPTION
    With a GL card fitted, the BIOS CONOUT MIRRORS every console byte onto
    the GL screen as well as the serial port, so the ROM monitor, the OS
    and every program appear on the display -- the "glass TTY" of two-mode
    operation (see the two-mode design). The mirror is ON BY DEFAULT
    whenever a card is present: the monitor switches it on at wake
    (GCONEN=1), installs the stroke font from /FONT.GL on the CF root, and
    blanks the screen, so the very first banner is already on the LCD.
    Serial always carries the same output, and input stays on serial.

    screen is the switch. `screen off` clears the GCONEN flag and the
    console goes back to serial only -- use it when you want a program's
    graphics left undisturbed by the prompt, or when a test needs a
    pristine framebuffer. `screen on` sets the flag and calls the GCLS
    BIOS to clear the screen and home the text cursor. With no argument
    screen reports on or off. The setting lasts for the session: `exit`
    back to the monitor re-runs its wake-up, which switches the mirror on
    again.

    A full-screen GL program still SUSPENDS the console while it draws
    (the GTSUSP flag) and quitting back to the shell resumes it. Resuming
    does NOT clear the screen: a program's last frame stays put and the
    prompt draws over it, so incremental drawing across commands -- `gl`
    chains, `tri ... k` scenes -- keeps working. When you want a clean
    console again, `screen on` gives you one.

    On a machine with no display, `screen on` is a no-op and prints
    "screen: no display fitted".

    Deferred: proper scrollback -- the console currently CLEARS and homes
    when it fills the bottom row -- and per-cell erase (backspace leaves
    the old glyph).

EXAMPLES
    screen off      keep the GL screen for graphics; console on serial only
    screen on       console back on the LCD, from a clean screen
    term            the Term app: an on-screen shell in the app frame

